{"networkAttributes":[{"KEGG_PATHWAY_IMAGE":"https://www.kegg.jp/kegg/pathway/spo/spo00860.png","KEGG_PATHWAY_ORG":"spo","modules_id":["Heme biosynthesis, animals and fungi, glycine => heme [PATH:spo00860]"],"KEGG_PATHWAY_ID":"path:spo00860","classes":["Metabolism","Metabolism of cofactors and vitamins"],"description":"Porphyrins are a group of chemical compounds with the backbone ring structure consisting of four linked pyrrole units. Metal complexes of porphyrins, as well as those of corrinoids with the one-carbon shorter ring structure, play important biological roles. They include iron-containing heme, magnesium-containing chlorophyll, nickel-containing coenzyme F430 and cobalt-containing cobamide (such as vitamin B12 coenzyme) as shown in this map.","diseases":[""],"diseases_id":[""],"KEGG_PATHWAY_LINK":"https://www.kegg.jp/kegg-bin/show_pathway?spo00860","modules":["spo_M00868"],"name":"Porphyrin metabolism [spo00860]_1"}]},
